(5) THE EXPENSIVE MODULI -- these force the adversary to spend a multiple
    moduli in [15,60] with blocking cost > 13 (or infinite): []
    count: 0 of 46

    Any family blocking ALL of these must contain, for each such q,
    a speed divisible by q.  With only 13 speeds that is a strong
    simultaneous-divisibility constraint -- and it is exactly the
    structure the THM-1105 lcm construction exploits.

(6) SANITY: does the cost prediction match the S375 counterexample?
    q=15: predicted cost ceil(phi/k) = 4 speeds
    speeds actually doing the blocking: [11, 77, 137, 226, 232, 262]
    number of distinct blockers: 7  (prediction was a LOWER bound)
(7) THE COUNTING THEOREM DOES FIRE -- just not at 13 speeds.
    THEOREM: if s speeds, none divisible by q, and s*k_q < phi(q),
    then some p/q is lonely (bad numerators < phi(q), so one survives).
    best modulus = the one maximising phi(q)/k_q:
      q=  90  phi=  24  k_q=  2  phi/k =  12.00  -> fires for s <= 11
      q=  23  phi=  22  k_q=  2  phi/k =  11.00  -> fires for s <= 10
      q= 150  phi=  40  k_q=  4  phi/k =  10.00  -> fires for s <= 9
      q=  66  phi=  20  k_q=  2  phi/k =  10.00  -> fires for s <= 9
      q=  41  phi=  40  k_q=  4  phi/k =  10.00  -> fires for s <= 9
      q=  25  phi=  20  k_q=  2  phi/k =  10.00  -> fires for s <= 9
      q= 126  phi=  36  k_q=  4  phi/k =   9.00  -> fires for s <= 8
      q=  54  phi=  18  k_q=  2  phi/k =   9.00  -> fires for s <= 8

    MAXIMUM s for which the counting proof works: s = 11  (at q = 90)
    LRC(14) needs s = 13.  The proof covers s <= 11, missing by 2.
    i.e. this argument closes the analogue for up to 12 runners and
    falls exactly 2 speeds short of the open case.
